WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.370
- [Instructor] We're told that
Kayla wants to visit a friend

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.610
who lives eight kilometers away.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.120
She'll ride the subway as far as she can

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.840
before walking the rest of the way.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.840
First, she needs to buy an
access pass that costs $5.50.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.970
There's also a fee of $1.25 per stop.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.960
This is an expensive subway.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.730
Kayla doesn't want to spend
more than $15 on the trip.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.660
So she wants to know the largest number

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.650
of stops she can afford.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.650
Let S represent the number
of stops that Kayla buys.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.710
So first, pause this video

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.350
and see if you can write an inequality

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.210
that describes how many stops,

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.730
or that describes the
situation that describes

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.640
that she wants to take
as many stops as she can,

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.103
but she doesn't wanna spend more than $15.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:52.910
All right, now let's do this together.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.280
So first, let's just
think about an expression

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.350
for how much she spends.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.630
So no matter what, she's
going to spend $5.50,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.663
so we can write it like this, so $5.50,

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.430
that's what she's going to spend,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.310
even if she doesn't take any stops.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.550
And it's $1.25 per stop, and
S is the number of stops.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.610
So the amount she's going
to spend just from the stops

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.970
is going to be $1.25 times S.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.170
So it's going to be plus $1.25 S.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.580
This is the upfront she has to spend,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.290
and this is how much she's
going to spend on stop.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.730
So this is an S right over here

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.070
and I wrote a five right next to it,

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.490
they look kind of similar.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.150
And we know that she doesn't
want to spend more than $15.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.230
So she's willing to spend up to $15.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.410
So this total amount that she spends

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.393
has to be less than or equal to $15.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.010
Or if we didn't write it
with the dollar symbols,

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:51.777
we would write 5.50 plus 1.25 S

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.160
is going to be less than or equal to,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.720
or needs to be less than or equal to 15.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.530
Now that we've written this inequality,

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:05.460
what is the number of stops
that Kayla can afford?

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.550
What's the largest number of
stops that she can afford?

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.210
Pause this video and
try to figure that out.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.580
Well, to figure that out,
we just have to solve for S

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.560
and then figure out what the largest S is

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.260
that satisfies the inequality
once we've solved for S.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.320
So the first thing I would do

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.453
is subtract 5.50 from both sides.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.933
When we do that, we are
left with 1.25 or $1.25 S

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:40.210
is less than or equal to 9.50.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.120
And then I would divide
both sides by 1.25.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.660
And since I'm dividing both
sides by a positive value,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.420
it doesn't change the
direction of the inequality,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.373
1.25 and then divide this by 1.25,

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:59.830
9.5 divide by 1.25

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.870
is equal to 7.6.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.737
So we get that S needs to be
less than or equal to 7.6.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.820
So we can't take a
fractional number of stops.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:20.220
So the largest number of
stops that Kayla can take

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.630
is going to be seven stops.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.463
She can't take eight,

00:03:22.463 --> 00:03:24.520
and she can't take 7 1/2 or 7.6.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.830
So the largest number she
can take is seven stops.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:32.830
So she can take as many

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:38.280
as seven stops.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.523
And we are done.

